meta {
  name: accept-order
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/transition
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "status": "accepted"
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    // Strict, because `order_uid` is reserved for this lifecycle -- nothing else in the collection
    // cancels or settles it, so the transition is always legal when this runs in sequence.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("the order is now accepted", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.status).to.equal("accepted")
  })

  test("and the next legal transitions come with it", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.next_statuses).to.be.an("array")
  })
}

docs {
  # Accept

Run these four in order -- the state machine refuses to skip states, so `mark-served` before
`accept-order` answers `TX_ORD_006` (409).

Validated under a row lock, which is what makes two staff phones tapping this in the same second
resolve to exactly one winner. The loser gets a 409 and the correct client response is always to
refetch (docs/DECISIONS.md D1).
}
